the death toll continues to rise in  pakistan from devastating floods  authorities report at least 50 deaths in  the deluge hit sindh province in recent  days more than 1200 people have died in  the past weeks  satellite imagery shows the scale of the  flooding which has affected about a  third of the country there are now  concerns the country's crop of wheat  could be wiped out affecting food supply  officials say the record monsoon rains  and melting glaciers are being caused by  climate change  pakistan has appealed for more  international aid and the country's  largest charity the eti foundation has  urged the government to reverse a ban on  many international ngos to allow them to  help  the evidence of formal lives lies  scattered throughout this vast rural  area now underwater  these used to be people's homes but now  are largely submerged or destroyed  those displaced by the floods are  flocking to this camp in the sindh  province searching for food medicine and  a place to stay  many of them don't have to think too  much about their priorities  the whole town is underwater  nothing could be saved  there's no food available no rice no  vegetables there's nothing here  all that the people worry about is food  many say they don't even care about  getting tents  only food  the camp has an improvised hospital  which is accepting people without charge  volunteer doctors try to do what they  can to relieve the suffering of the sick  we provide cannula and drip as most  patients who come here suffer from  diarrhea malaria high grade fever and  skin problems  at our outpatient department five to six  doctors treat 700 to 800 patients every  day  these makeshift tents are filling up  quickly with most of the locals having  nowhere else to go  setting up for an even more uncertain  life they briefly sum up what happened  to them in the last few weeks  there was a hundred percent loss for our  crops  nothing could be saved as far as the  crops are concerned  rice  cotton vegetables and fruits  all have been destroyed  as the aid groups step up their efforts  they as well as pakistan's government  are appealing to the world for more help  for the devastated communities  but for now all that these desperate  people can do is to stick to such camps  on the few remaining patches of dry land  we'll go to islamabad now and speak to  adil sharaz the country director at the  aid organization care international in  pakistan uh thank you for joining us  you just returned from the southwestern  province of balochistan which has been  hit by the worst of the flooding what  was the situation like there  i would say the situation is extremely  challenging because uh a large  population is affected from this flood  uh during our recent uh visit to the  different parts of pakistan including  balochistan and sin we have seen  villages are washed away and there is a  large destruction which has happened out  of this flooding uh in terms of uh crops  which are destroyed  uh the bridges which are washed away  roads are washed away and also people  have lost shelter livestock and  livelihood on which they were dependent  so  there are affected area which has been  hard hit from this flooding and there's  a large population who have lost all the  basic things which they need to live a  healthy life  and the authorities are reporting 33  million people affected 90 percent of  them haven't received any assistance yet  what's most needed in terms of aid and  how does it get to people  the area in terms of geographical  coverage is quite big like we are  talking about 33 million people who are  affected at least 72 districts are  affected in this emergency so this is  really a large coverage we are talking  about uh  more and more aid organization and  government is reaching out to these  affected areas and providing assistance  but we all need to understand we are  talking about a large population at this  stage  the most important needs are food  uh the shelter which is required  at the same time sanitation facilities  are either very limited or they don't  exist  in terms of like you know gender in  emergency organization like care is  working a lot on that we do see  emergency in terms of what are the  gender needs in that area and we do feel  a lot of like support which is required  for the female population in the  affected area including making sure that  they get the ministerial hygiene kit  they need  sanitation facilities which are near to  where they are living so that it can  minimize the risk of security  we also need to ensure that there is  clean drinking water available in this  area we are hearing and also witnessing  more and more water bond diseases which  are coming including  diarrhea malaria skin diseases and  others  and all of that require an immediate  support and need aid workers like us and  also other organization are reaching out  to these affected population  but at the same time logistically this  operation is challenging because of  still water in certain area which is  making uh supplies and aid workers to  reach out to these population in in a  more time as compared to before well sir  it sounds like an incredibly challenging  and tragic situation given that um  why isn't the government allowing some  of these banned foreign ngos from  working again in the country  there are a large number of  international organization already in  country and the government i would say  have been very positive about giving  them permission to work in these  affected areas  there is a mechanism available uh to  allow apply and allow for the new  organization to come in country and i  think as as long as they can complete  the requirement there will be more and  more organization can come into the  country but organization like us we have  seen with the positive response from the  government side in terms of providing  access in terms of providing permission  to work in these affected population  areas  so i can say fairly that organizations  are getting support which are in country  uh the new organization organization who  are working before in country uh they  need to just use the right channel to  apply for permission to work in country  and i'm sure there will be facilitation  from the government side okay message  received adil sharaz thanks so much  thank you so much  you
 

